# Core §15 — A HOST-LANGUAGE NAME IS AN ORDINARY ID.
#
# `constructor`, `toString`, `valueOf`, `hasOwnProperty` and `__proto__` are
# legal ids by the id rule (core §1): nothing in the grammar reserves them.
# A JavaScript engine that keys an id, a class name or an option value into a
# plain object reads them as the object's inherited members instead. The
# reference engine did, until this release: `node constructor` plus any edge
# threw, a lone one drew `viewBox="0 0 NaN NaN"`, and a `__proto__` key
# reached the prototype every later render in the process shares.
#
# So this file uses each of them where an author's word is a key: node ids,
# a group id with `in=`, a class id with `class=`, both ends of an edge, a
# `rank` list and a `pin`. It must parse, render, and render the same bytes
# twice, exactly as it would with any other five words. The refusal half
# (the same words where they are NOT a keyword or an enum value) is
# `961-errors-prototype-names-unknown-words`.
figdown 0.5 block
title "Prototype names are ordinary ids"
class valueOf "hot"
group hasOwnProperty "G"
node constructor "C" class=valueOf in=hasOwnProperty
node toString "T"
node __proto__ "P" in=hasOwnProperty
node b "B"
edge constructor -> toString
edge toString -> __proto__
edge __proto__ -> b
edge b -> constructor
rank toString,b
layout
pin b at=(700,40)
pin __proto__ at=(400,160)
